      "claim": "This gravitational force acts continuously on Earth and is directed toward the Sun.",
      "evidence": "Gravitational force is a central force pointing along the line joining the two masses.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "An object moving in a circular (or elliptical) orbit requires a centripetal force equal to m·v²/r.",
      "evidence": "Centripetal acceleration a_c = v²/r; force needed = m·a_c.",
      "weight": 0.2,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "At Earth’s orbital distance the Sun’s gravitational force magnitude equals the required centripetal force for Earth’s orbital speed (~30 km/s).",
      "evidence": "Compute a_g = G·M_sun / r² ≈ 0.006 m/s²; compute a_c = v²/r with v≈30 km/s gives ≈0.006 m/s²; forces match.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore the Sun’s gravitational pull provides the centripetal force that keeps Earth in orbit around the Sun.",
      "evidence": "Step 4 shows equality of forces; the only sustained inward force is the Sun’s gravity.",
      "weight": 0.1,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
